  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWith current sfc analysis showing the weakened low pressure system \n(the remnants from the departed nor'easter) and the upper level \ntrough to our east, the chances for any lingering isolated showers \nacross the region has decreased for the rest of the day, however \nthere could still be some patchy light drizzle or mist, mainly \naround the Capital Region. with what lingering moisture's left and \nweak lifting forces, but should end around sunset. Drier and more \nwarm air will begin to filter in overnight tonight from the \nwest/northwest along with a upper level positively tilted ridge to \nbuild in. Sinking air will help to create some areas of partial \nclearing tonight with just enough for patchy fog to develop across \nmuch of the region, especially in the more sheltered valley areas. \nWith winds and upper level flow shifting more \nsoutherly/southwesterly tomorrow, a dry day is expected however \nmoisture will continue to be advected into the region from the Gulf \nkeeping dewpoints in the 60s and skies partly cloudy to cloudy with \nbrief breaks of sun. Warm air advection and rising heights will make \nfor above normal high temps across the region with the NAEFs \nEnsemble guidance +1 to +2 STDEV being in the upper 60s (terrain) \nand mid 70s (valleys). This trend will continue again with \nThursday's highs being similar to tomorrow and Friday's highs \nreaching into the low 70s (terrain) and low 80s (valleys). \nProbabilistic guidance has about a 70-80% chance of >75F in valley \nareas and a 60-70% chance of >80F in southern portions of the Mid-\nHudson Valley on Friday. While a few showers are possible in the \nsouthern ADKs Thursday afternoon, mainly dry and cloudy conditions \nare expected for the rest of the region with the next chance for \nmore widespread rain arriving Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure slides to our east throughout the day Thursday as an \nupper level shortwave along with strengthening upper level winds \nmove into the region by Friday morning. A cold front ahead of the \ntrough will bring the next chance of rain into the region. Southerly \nflow will continue to supply enough moisture into the region with \nPWATs increasing to 1.5 to 2\", about +1.25 to +1.75 STDEV above \nnormal Friday. The latest models currently have scattered rain \nshowers arriving Friday late morning from northwest to southeast. \nWidespread rainfall totals up to 0.25\" are expected with localized \nup to 0.5\" possible. Probabilistic guidance has a 20 to 30% chance \nfor >0.25\" for areas I-90 south and a 40 to 60% chance for areas \nnorth and west of Albany and in higher terrain areas like the \nsouther ADKs and southern Greens. Chances for >0.50\" or greater is \nless than 30%. With strong flow aloft keeping showers progressing \nand limited instability <300 J/Kg, showers should mainly be light to \nmoderate, however brief heavier bursts could still occur before \nexiting Friday night. Flooding issues are not anticipated. After a \nmainly dry and seasonable Saturday with highs expected to be in the \n60s and up to 70 in the Mid Hudson Valley, additional rainfall is \npossible Saturday night into Sunday morning as multiple shortwaves \npass through, however confidence is low at this time as high \npressure over the Great Lakes could keep much of the showers south \nof the region. Overall, rain this weekend looks to be beneficial \nwith no hydro concerns at this time. Looking ahead, drier and below \nnormal temperatures are expected for the start of next week.",
